### What Changed and Why

#### 🔒 `helmet` — Security Headers
Adds ~14 HTTP response headers in one call (e.g., `X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options`, `Strict-Transport-Security`). Without it, browsers apply no extra protections against common attacks.

#### 🌐 CORS Whitelist
The original code applies no CORS policy, meaning any origin can make credentialed requests in permissive browser configs. An explicit whitelist loaded from an environment variable is the safe default.

#### 🛡️ Rate Limiting
Without `express-rate-limit`, every endpoint is open to unlimited requests per second. A 100-requests/15-min window is a reasonable starting point.

#### 📦 Body Size Limit
`express.json({ limit: '10kb' })` prevents payload-based DoS attacks where an attacker sends a multi-megabyte JSON body.

#### ✅ Input Validation on Query Params
The `limit` query parameter is now parsed, validated as a bounded integer, and returns a descriptive `400` if malformed — preventing unexpected behavior or injection through query strings.

#### 🙈 Field Filtering / Data Minimization
Using destructuring (`{ passwordHash, ...safe }`) ensures sensitive fields are never accidentally sent to clients, even if the data model changes.

#### 🚨 Global Error Handler (4-arg middleware)
Express's special 4-argument middleware `(err, req, res, next)` is the correct pattern for centralized error handling. It prevents unhandled errors from crashing the process or leaking stack traces.

#### 🔧 Environment-Driven Port & Mode
`process.env.PORT` is standard for cloud deployments (Heroku, Railway, AWS). `NODE_ENV` controls whether verbose debug info is exposed.

